          "question": "I have been on a proton pump inhibitor for six years. Is long-term use a concern, what does guidance describe about review, and how is reflux coded?",
          "answer": "Long-term use of a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) can be associated with lower vitamin B12 levels, and guidance advises periodic measurement of these levels (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy).\n\nFor the initial management of typical reflux symptoms, guidance describes an empiric eight-week trial of a PPI, which implies a review after this initial period (guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features). For long-term use, beyond the B12 level monitoring mentioned, general review guidance for PPIs was not extensively covered in the retrieved passages. You should discuss the need for ongoing review of your medication with your clinician (consultation-specialist). Explicit detailed guidance on the ongoing review process for long-term PPI therapy beyond the initial trial period is not provided in the current information (research-specialist).\n\nRegarding how reflux is coded, gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is classified under code K21 in the ICD-10 system. Additionally, the status of long-term drug therapy is coded from the factors-influencing-health chapter (coding-gerd-k21).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
